{
  "statement": "Those who urge California's public universities to rush AI adoption without deliberative rule-making argue that speed is the only defense against competitive decline. They say pilot programs can test tools safely, and that human judgment is already so flawed that automated assistance can only improve outcomes. \n\nThat argument ignores three structural realities. First, AI-driven deskilling hollows out the very expertise the university exists to cultivate. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people in 2018 and 2019 because the Federal Aviation Administration delegated critical safety certification to the manufacturer, training pilots to trust automated systems that could fatally override them. In medicine, diagnostic AI systems have caused clinicians to lose independent diagnostic skills, leading to errors when the tools were unavailable or wrong. Education follows the same trajectory: when students and instructors offload reasoning to algorithmic tools, they atrophy the critical thinking that protects them from being misled by a flawed output. The cultivation of independent human minds is the university's core mission; deploying AI without verified safeguards is an act of institutional self-sabotage.\n\nSecond, absent ex ante rule-making, no named actor bears responsibility when AI harms a student. The United Kingdom's 2020 A-level algorithm downgraded 39% of teacher-predicted grades and disproportionately penalized students from disadvantaged backgrounds, throwing thousands of university admissions into chaos—and no official was ever held accountable. In 2023, Turnitin's AI detection feature falsely flagged original student writing as machine-generated at multiple institutions, yet students found no clear appeals path. In California, the moment a CSU or UC campus deploys an algorithmic grading or admissions tool without a defined accountability chain, it creates a governance vacuum where a wrongly expelled student or an unfairly denied applicant has no recourse. That violates the university's fiduciary duty to its students and faculty. The fix is not to freeze all technology; it is to name the responsible officer, establish an independent audit mechanism, and preserve faculty veto rights before any tool touches a classroom.\n\nThird, competitive pressure to adopt AI quickly is a trap that rewards corner-cutting and punishes the institutions that maintain rigorous standards. When universities raced to deploy remote proctoring software during the 2020 pandemic, faculty and students at UC and CSU campuses faced privacy violations, biased facial recognition, and psychological distress—followed by lawsuits and faculty revolts. The institutions that had the fastest adoption rates suffered the greatest reputational damage. A rush to implement unvetted AI tools now will produce similar scandals, while the campus that insists on proven safety mechanisms will attract the faculty and students most committed to genuine learning. The California Master Plan for Higher Education enshrined shared governance as the safeguard of academic integrity. Preserving that structure against the pressure to chase market signals is not obstructionism; it is the only path to durable institutional strength.\n\nThese conclusions rest on two premises: that the university's duty of care toward human judgment is as weighty as aviation's duty toward passenger safety, and that enforceable oversight can be built through existing shared governance bodies. Reject these premises, and we are left gambling that unaccountable software will never make a consequential mistake. That is exactly the gamble the FAA took—and 346 people paid with their lives.",
