  "statement": "The safetyist's strongest case deserves to be taken seriously, not dismissed. They point to Boeing's 737 MAX, where automation deskilled pilots and killed people. They point to the UK's A-level algorithm, which crushed students' futures without a single person being fired. They point to 2020's remote proctoring debacle, where top-down AI adoption burned institutional trust overnight. The insight is real: when decision-making authority is severed from frontline expertise, and when no one is on the hook for harm, bad things happen. These aren't hypothetical fears; they're documented institutional failures. The tension that emerges is not about whether we want accountability or safety—it's about whether pre-approval rules actually produce them. In each of those cases, the disaster unfolded under existing regulations and named offices. The rules gave cover; they didn't prevent catastrophe.\n\nNow sharpen the skeptic's challenge. The evidence that AI tools enhance learning in higher education is thin. Most studies are short-term, small-sample, and unpublished. Pushing these tools into classrooms risks turning 430,000 UC and CSU students into involuntary test subjects. The skeptic says: first prove it works, then deploy. That's not obstructionism; it's fidelity to the university's educational mission. The argument has moral weight. But it contains an overlooked cost: every semester we wait, graduates enter an AI-saturated labor market without the cognitive augmentation their competitors already use. That's not caution; it's passive harm inflicted at scale. The real question is whether we freeze adoption until perfect evidence arrives, or run structured pilots that generate that evidence while students still benefit. If the evidence is shallow, the answer isn't to stop—it's to accelerate the research by running more pilots, faster, with rigorous measurement built in from day one.\n\nCalifornia's public universities are losing the competitive race right now. The state's tech sector employs over 1.8 million people, and AI-related job postings are growing at double-digit rates annually. The UC system's own workforce projections state that AI literacy will be a baseline requirement for high-skill employment inside five years. The Bay Area Council Economic Institute documents firms bypassing degree programs that haven't integrated AI into their curricula. This isn't a future scenario; it's a live trend punishing students whose professors can't get approval to use the tools. The moral calculus flips the safetyist frame: delaying adoption is the choice that inflicts mass harm—on students who will compete for jobs against AI-fluent peers, on the state that depends on their productivity, on the public mission of universities to prepare people for the economy they will face.\n\nAI is cognitive augmentation, not deskilling, when faculty—not procurement offices—design the integration. Meta-analyses from medicine and law show that AI-assisted professionals outperform unaided peers while maintaining, and sometimes improving, diagnostic accuracy. The UCSD AI writing assistant trial, though preliminary, points in the same direction. The safetyist's descent into the 737 MAX assumes delegation without retention of expert judgment. But universities can mandate a different architecture: tools that explain their reasoning, prompt students to critique outputs, and make the cognitive process visible to instructors. That's the opposite of deskilling; it's metacognitive strengthening. And we can build accountability in motion, not in advance. CSU's Chancellor's Office already allows campus-level AI pilots through interim guidelines, pairing faculty oversight committees with real-time data dashboards. Early internal reviews show fewer compliance incidents than systemwide rule-writers feared. When a biased advising tool surfaces, the dashboard flags it within days, not months, and the faculty committee corrects it. That's a tighter feedback loop than any static rulebook written in 2024 will provide in 2026.\n\nThe remote proctoring backlash wasn't caused by speed; it was caused by top-down coercion that bypassed shared governance. Faculty autonomy isn't a brake on adoption—it's the engine that makes adoption stick. The CSU model demonstrates this: departments design their own AI integration pilots, preserving academic freedom while aligning with workforce goals. When instructors choose the tools and shape the pedagogy, they don't resist innovation; they drive it. The cost of freezing now falls on the students who walk into a job market that didn't wait for committee minutes. Every year of deliberation is a cohort of graduates less prepared than their peers from institutions that moved. Who gave anyone the right to make that choice for them? The burden of proof is on those who would halt deployment: show me a harm that iterative, transparent pilots can't detect and fix faster than a pre-approval committee can schedule its second meeting, and then we can talk about pausing.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Every day of delay is a cohort of students who lose competitive ground—an hourglass measuring not just time but opportunity cost."},
    {"symbol": "🧠", "tooltip": "AI as cognitive augmentation, when faculty-led, strengthens critical thinking rather than deskilling it—the brain, not the brake."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "California's public universities are losing the competitive race right now—1.8 million tech jobs, double-digit AI job growth, and students being bypassed by employers because curricula haven't kept pace.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": ""
    },
    {
      "claim": "The safetyist's descent into the 737 MAX assumes delegation without retention of expert judgment, but universities can mandate tools that explain reasoning and prompt critique, strengthening metacognition instead of eroding it.",
      "targets": ["AN-3"],
      "relationship": "concedes_and_pivots"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The CSU Chancellor's Office's interim guidelines, pairing faculty oversight committees with real-time data dashboards, show that accountability can be built into rapid deployment—not held hostage by static rule-making.",
      "targets": ["AN-3"],
      "relationship": "specifies"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Remote proctoring's reputational fire wasn't caused by speed; it was caused by top-down coercion that bypassed shared governance—faculty autonomy is the engine of adoption, not its brake.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": ""
