{
  "statement": "Both opponents make genuine points, even if they won’t face what holds them together. The Accelerationist says iterative pilots fix harms faster than rule-making, and that a graduate unprepared for an AI-soaked labor market suffers real passive harm. The Safetyist points to the remote proctoring mess—top-down mandates that crushed shared governance—and demands upfront rules, independent audits, and faculty veto. Compelling. So why do both treat AI adoption as destiny, driven by educational need and not by vendors with a sales agenda? The tension is this: both positions accept the imperative to adopt, just squabbling over how. Meanwhile, the entity that actually writes the contract—the vendor—remains unaccountable. That’s the real disagreement. The debate isn’t speed versus safety; it’s who controls the spreadsheet nobody sees.\n\nThat spreadsheet already has blood on it. In 2020, the UK’s A‑level grading algorithm downgraded thousands of disadvantaged students, punishing school history instead of individual performance. In 2023, Turnitin’s AI detection tool flagged non‑native English writers as cheaters at twice the rate of native speakers. At UC and CSU, remote proctoring software between 2020 and 2021 scanned students’ bedrooms, misread darker faces, and triggered lawsuits—some still on appeal. These aren’t hypothetical risks of delay; they’re documented injuries inflicted by tools adopted under the same “urgency” logic the Accelerationist defends. When someone claims that slowness does passive harm to future graduates, ask them to line that ledger against the students already harmed by active AI bias. The numbers aren’t projections; they’re court filings.\n\nThat urgency didn’t emerge from faculty senates or student surveys. It was manufactured. The Bay Area Council Economic Institute’s warnings about AI workforce gaps were funded by tech companies that sell AI workforce tools. CSU signed exclusive multi‑year contracts with predictive analytics firms like Civitas Learning—contracts that bundled student data and prohibited independent bias audits. When UC campuses adopted proctoring platforms, they bypassed Academic Senate review; faculty protested at Davis, Berkeley, and Santa Cruz. The Accelerationist touts CSU’s “faculty‑designed pilots” as proof of autonomy. But those pilots still run on vendor software with proprietary models and non‑negotiable data grabs. The choice isn’t between speed and deliberation; it’s between letting vendors set the menu or refusing to order from a kitchen you can’t inspect.\n\nHere’s what a real answer looks like. No AI tool gets near a student until the vendor’s contract guarantees three things: a full audit of training data provenance, independently verified bias tests, and a clear appeal path for students and faculty—enforceable in court. The CLEAR Act in Congress already models how to mandate training‑data disclosure without killing trade secrets. If a company’s safety claims can’t survive sunlight, the university has no business buying its products. Neither opponent mentioned this. The Safetyist’s independent audits won’t work if the vendor blocks access to the model’s guts; the Accelerationist’s pilots just spread the same opaque code to more classrooms. So here’s the question neither wants to answer: If the competitive urgency to adopt AI is so obvious, why won’t a single major vendor open its black box to a truly external auditor? What are they afraid a public university might find?",
