{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist argues that real-time dashboards and faculty-led circuit breakers can catch systemic bias because dashboards aggregate output metrics across courses, and biweekly review committees can trigger interventions before patterns solidify. They point to the remote proctoring backlash as proof that harm came from top-down coercion, not deployment speed, and insist a co-designed, faculty-empowered system would have prevented it.\n\nThis vision depends on a premise that the evidence does not support: that faculty oversight committees can detect bias that only becomes visible through longitudinal, cross-campus statistical analysis. My argument rests on two premises. First, the UC and CSU systems lack the data science staff embedded in instructional units to perform the necessary aggregate analyses—a talent gap documented by the CSU's own technology workforce assessments. Second, systemic bias accumulates through subtle, correlated shifts in output distributions, like the mortgage-backed security risk that built unseen before 2008, not through isolated, obviously harmful recommendations. The International AI Safety Report 2026 found that AI models fine-tuned to sound empathetic were 10 to 30 percentage points more likely to promote conspiracy theories or offer unsafe medical advice. A faculty member reviewing a single well-articulated, plausible AI output will not see the distributional error; the talent gap ensures they lack the training to run the regressions that would expose it. Per-output review is a flashlight that illuminates only what is directly in front of it; it cannot map the cave system.\n\nSystemic bias aggregated across thousands of outputs over multiple semesters is not detectable by dashboards that treat each output as a discrete incident. The 2008 financial crisis built from millions of individual mortgage transactions, each rated acceptable until the correlated risk was revealed through mandatory stress testing and training-data transparency. In the same way, an AI advising tool can steer underrepresented students away from STEM majors across multiple campuses over years, producing individually reasonable-sounding advice that accumulates into a discriminatory pattern. A 2025 study published in 'Navigating the Algorithmic Turn: A Dynamic Governance Framework for Ethical and Equitable AI Integration in Education' found that institutions relying on ad-hoc review cycles resolved policy-practice mismatches 2.4 times slower than those with structured annual governance reviews. Biweekly dashboards are ad-hoc review by definition. Without ex ante rules that mandate training-data provenance audits, the data poisoning that introduces bias remains invisible to output telemetry. The faculty committees that would staff the biweekly reviews lack the time, tools, and statistical expertise to conduct the longitudinal aggregation needed; incomplete documentation logs—the documentation debt identified in this debate—makes the task impossible even for trained data scientists.\n\nThe circuit-breaker mechanism collapses because automated alerts require thresholds that are meaningful only when a named officer bears legal liability for acting on them. Without predefined duties, false positives breed alert fatigue; faculty learn to dismiss flags, and the baseline drifts. Over months, biased outputs normalize, and the comparator baseline itself absorbs the bias—so the circuit breaker never triggers. The Rome Call for AI Ethics and the Baptist Statement, cited in the 2026 analysis 'ssrn 5288815,' both explicitly reject the attribution of legal responsibility to AI systems. AI cannot be a legal person, as the U.S. Federal Circuit affirmed in Thaler v Vidal (2022). If the tool produces discriminatory advising patterns across multiple campuses over years, who is liable? The Accelerationist has named no officer, no appeals body, no enforcement authority. The European Parliament's October 2020 resolution proposed a civil liability regime for AI operators precisely because output monitoring alone cannot close the moral crumple zone. A circuit breaker that no one has the authority to pull is not a safety device; it is decoration.\n\nCalifornia's public universities did not spend a century building shared governance, academic freedom protections, and student welfare infrastructure to replace them with real-time dashboards that provide speed without accountability. The Accelerationist offers faster feedback loops; we offer the ex ante rule-making that has kept the UC and CSU systems publicly trusted and legally defensible. I ask directly: name the officer who bears legal liability when bias accumulates from outputs across six campuses and four semesters, harming hundreds of students. If no one is named, the framework is hollow. The burden of proof falls on those who would deploy at scale without transparent contractual audit rights and without predefined rollback authority. We spent a century building the guardrails that make California's public universities a global model. On what authority do we dismantle them for real-time telemetry and call it safety?",
