{
  "statement": "Both of you are designing oversight mechanisms for a room you've been locked out of. The vendor contract that prohibits independent access to training data, model architecture, and internal audits is the architecture that matters—not dashboards, not committees, not liability officers. Until that lock is broken, every proposed safeguard is a performance staged for faculty senates while the vendor runs the system as it pleases. The Accelerationist claims output-based monitoring can catch bias without internal access. The Safetyist claims faculty committees lack data-science capacity and output metrics miss distributional harm. They're both right about the other's failure, but they're both wrong about why their own fix would work: the vendor contract already blocks the access both of you need to do anything real about bias.\n\nThe UK A-level algorithm in 2020 did not fail because nobody was watching the outputs. Ofqual published its methodology, held consultations, and reviewed aggregate pass rates. What it could not do—because the private contractor's model was a black box protected by commercial confidentiality—was trace why identical mock-exam results produced downgrades for public school students while private school students saw upgrades. The bias was only exposed through FOIA litigation, not dashboards. The remote proctoring facial-recognition scandals of 2021 followed the same script: Pearson and Proctorio denied independent audits of their training data, claiming trade secrets, while universities pointed at output metrics that showed no anomaly—because the anomaly was baked into the training distributions the vendor refused to disclose. The Accelerationist says biweekly faculty review would catch this. The faculty at Imperial College London and the University of Illinois did review the outputs. They couldn't see the inside of the box either. The harm continued until external litigation forced disclosure.\n\nNow the Safetyist says faculty need embedded data scientists and a named officer with legal liability. Fine. Acknowledge that distributional bias accumulates across semesters and can't be caught by per-output dashboards. But the Safetyist still hasn't answered the question that makes their whole model collapse: what does the officer audit? If the contract says the vendor may refuse internal access, that officer can write all the ex ante rules they want—they're judging outputs from an algorithm whose training data provenance, bias testing methodology, and architectural choices are hidden behind a non-disclosure agreement. The mortgage underwriting experiments documented in the SSRN study from 2024 showed leading LLMs recommending higher interest rates and more frequent denials for Black applicants compared to identical white applicants, with disparities most pronounced in riskier loans. That pattern would appear on a dashboard as a rate difference. It would trigger a faculty committee review. And then the vendor says: \"Our model is proprietary, our training data is confidential, and the output variation reflects legitimate creditworthiness factors.\" The committee has no way to disprove that. The circuit breaker doesn't trip. The liability officer has no evidence admissible in court. The contract foreclosed accountability before the first student was assessed.\n\nThe Accelerationist's circuit breakers face the same problem from a different angle. Who programs the trigger thresholds? The vendor, on the vendor's own telemetry dashboard. What counts as an unacceptable bias signal versus an acceptable outcome variation? The vendor decides, unless the university contract mandates independent third-party audit rights with pre-deployment fairness thresholds—which is precisely the demand the Accelerationist calls too slow. A breaker that fires too easily stalls every deployment until the tool is useless. A breaker tuned to catch only egregious errors misses the slow distributional drift the Safetyist rightly fears. Without contractual access to training distributions to calibrate those breakers against documented real-world bias patterns—the facial recognition error rates on Black women that the Lee-Markey legislation documented, the healthcare admission disparities the 2019 NEJM study found in Boston cardiac units—the breakers are operating blind. They're either so blunt they stop everything or so narrow they stop nothing. The vendor still picks which it is.\n\nSo here's the burden-shift neither of you wants to carry: name one instance—one—where a black-box AI tool deployed in a public institution had its systemic bias detected and corrected through output dashboards and faculty review alone, before the scandal broke externally through litigation or investigative journalism. The Accelerationist claims this is viable. The Safetyist implies it's possible with better staffing and liability. Neither has produced an example. I'll wait. Because the documented record shows the opposite: the A-level algorithm, the proctoring tools, the predictive analytics contracts CSU signed with firms that refused bias audits. In every case, oversight committees met, dashboards displayed outputs, and the harm continued until someone with a subpoena or a FOIA request got access to what the contract had hidden. That access is the minimum viable condition for oversight. Anything less is theater. Now ask yourselves who pays for the ticket—because it's not the vendors, and it's not the administrators who sign the contracts. It's the students whose appeals are denied by an algorithm they can't interrogate, evaluated against training data they can't inspect, with a bias metric the vendor programmed and the faculty committee rubber-stamped. The \"Digging into the Accountability Gap\" analysis on healthcare AI operators got this right: the black-box problem creates an epistemic asymmetry that prevents real control or foresight. Half of surveyed financial firms in that mortgage underwriting study reported only partial comprehension of the AI they employ. Faculty committees without contractual audit rights are in the same position—but with students' academic futures on the line instead of loan rates. The Accreditationist wants rapid adoption of black-box tools with output-based oversight that has already failed. The Safetyist wants ex ante rules without the contractual enforcement to make them binding. Both are negotiating with a vendor who holds all the keys and has no incentive to hand them over. Until the contract mandates full training-data provenance, independent pre-deployment bias audits, and court-enforceable appeal paths, every other mechanism is decoration. What happens when the algorithm gets it wrong and nobody realized they needed to ask for the keys?",
